1
00:00:01,800 --> 00:00:05,701
Independent hotels hand about two thirds of their bookings

2
00:00:05,771 --> 00:00:08,139
to Booking.com and Expedia.

3
00:00:08,743 --> 00:00:12,377
And they pay eighteen to thirty percent commission

4
00:00:12,458 --> 00:00:13,259
to do it.

5
00:00:13,758 --> 00:00:17,903
On a twenty room hotel, seventy percent occupancy...

6
00:00:18,495 --> 00:00:21,096
that's roughly a hundred thirty five thousand dollars a

7
00:00:21,177 --> 00:00:21,595
year.

8
00:00:22,036 --> 00:00:22,884
My estimate.

9
00:00:23,836 --> 00:00:25,763
From their own published rates.

10
00:00:26,613 --> 00:00:28,610
Okay so this is direct booking recovery.

11
00:00:28,703 --> 00:00:29,713
That's the thesis.

12
00:00:30,038 --> 00:00:31,814
You install and run the stack that gets a

13
00:00:31,872 --> 00:00:35,402
hotel's own website booking rooms, instead of Booking.com or

14
00:00:35,460 --> 00:00:37,364
Expedia doing it for a cut.

15
00:00:37,689 --> 00:00:39,106
Rate parity monitoring.

16
00:00:39,709 --> 00:00:41,079
A real booking engine.

17
00:00:41,358 --> 00:00:42,763
A missed call system.

18
00:00:43,088 --> 00:00:45,665
A re-book flow back to past guests.

19
00:00:45,990 --> 00:00:48,695
That's the plumbing, and one person can run it

20
00:00:48,730 --> 00:00:50,460
for a dozen properties at once.

21
00:00:50,785 --> 00:00:51,749
Who's it for?

22
00:00:52,132 --> 00:00:55,429
Independent hotels in the ten to forty room band.

23
00:00:55,940 --> 00:00:58,158
Big enough that the commission is real money.

24
00:00:58,367 --> 00:01:01,211
Small enough that nobody on staff actually owns revenue

25
00:01:01,246 --> 00:01:01,861
management.

26
00:01:02,558 --> 00:01:05,809
Five years ago, this required a channel-manager contract and

27
00:01:05,844 --> 00:01:07,329
a consultant on retainer.

28
00:01:07,770 --> 00:01:08,386
Now?

29
00:01:08,896 --> 00:01:11,799
Mews, Cloudbeds, SiteMinder...

30
00:01:12,612 --> 00:01:14,063
they run the parity checks.

31
00:01:14,342 --> 00:01:16,629
And a voice agent answers the phone the front

32
00:01:16,675 --> 00:01:17,720
desk can't get to.

33
00:01:18,045 --> 00:01:20,251
Nobody on staff was watching that number.

34
00:01:20,530 --> 00:01:23,815
And here's the thing, that wasn't negligence.

35
00:01:23,931 --> 00:01:26,393
A small property leans on the OTAs for a

36
00:01:26,509 --> 00:01:27,171
reason.

37
00:01:27,450 --> 00:01:29,597
You don't have the manpower to take it all

38
00:01:29,679 --> 00:01:30,027
in.

39
00:01:30,352 --> 00:01:33,266
And you don't have the skillset to build direct.

40
00:01:33,591 --> 00:01:34,799
And direct booking?

41
00:01:35,008 --> 00:01:36,134
It's not a website.

42
00:01:36,308 --> 00:01:38,607
Build it and they will come...

43
00:01:39,420 --> 00:01:40,546
that's not true here.

44
00:01:40,662 --> 00:01:41,498
It's marketing.

45
00:01:42,101 --> 00:01:44,458
Putting yourself out there, continuously.

46
00:01:44,969 --> 00:01:47,558
That's the job nobody had time for.

47
00:01:47,883 --> 00:01:50,727
And that's the job the agents are actually for.

48
00:01:50,855 --> 00:01:51,668
Not answering.

49
00:01:51,993 --> 00:01:52,748
Outreach.

50
00:01:52,864 --> 00:01:53,607
Profile.

51
00:01:53,816 --> 00:01:54,454
Follow-up.

52
00:01:54,698 --> 00:01:55,940
Your own growth engine.

53
00:01:56,184 --> 00:01:57,775
Not a better voicemail.

54
00:01:58,625 --> 00:02:01,284
Cloudbeds, they sell the software that's supposed to fix

55
00:02:01,342 --> 00:02:03,246
this, so take it for what it's worth.

56
00:02:04,058 --> 00:02:07,936
They looked at ninety million bookings for their 2026

57
00:02:07,971 --> 00:02:09,782
State of Independent Hotels report.

58
00:02:10,479 --> 00:02:14,646
Found that independents lose 63.4 percent of reservations

59
00:02:14,716 --> 00:02:15,831
to OTAs.

60
00:02:16,214 --> 00:02:19,755
Now add in Booking.com's Genius program and Visibility

61
00:02:19,825 --> 00:02:22,379
Booster, or Expedia's Accelerator.

62
00:02:22,762 --> 00:02:25,490
The real commission runs 18 to 30 percent.

63
00:02:25,699 --> 00:02:28,520
That's well above the 15 to 25 headline rate

64
00:02:28,567 --> 00:02:30,064
hotels think they're paying.

65
00:02:30,343 --> 00:02:34,209
Same dataset: OTA bookings cancel at 21.8

66
00:02:34,279 --> 00:02:37,599
percent, almost double the 10.6 percent you get on

67
00:02:37,646 --> 00:02:38,574
direct bookings.

68
00:02:38,900 --> 00:02:40,978
So the commission doesn't just cost money.

69
00:02:41,222 --> 00:02:43,752
It buys you a less reliable booking too.

70
00:02:44,194 --> 00:02:45,691
Let's do the arithmetic.

71
00:02:46,016 --> 00:02:48,663
And I want to be clear, this part's an

72
00:02:48,733 --> 00:02:50,509
estimate, not a reported figure.

73
00:02:50,788 --> 00:02:54,549
20 rooms, $180 average rate, 70

74
00:02:54,619 --> 00:02:55,652
percent occupancy.

75
00:02:56,163 --> 00:02:59,100
That books around $920,000 a year.

76
00:02:59,344 --> 00:03:02,084
Two thirds of that goes through OTAs at 22

77
00:03:02,142 --> 00:03:03,152
percent all in.

78
00:03:03,431 --> 00:03:06,008
That's about $135,000 gone.

79
00:03:06,333 --> 00:03:08,539
At Coqui Coqui, the first thing I did was

80
00:03:08,586 --> 00:03:10,908
get the brand and the site into good shape.

81
00:03:11,349 --> 00:03:12,997
Then came the harder part.

82
00:03:13,439 --> 00:03:15,993
Making the whole flow hold together.

83
00:03:16,271 --> 00:03:19,255
Social to the website, the website to a call

84
00:03:19,348 --> 00:03:22,239
or a booking, the booking through to check-in.

85
00:03:22,680 --> 00:03:26,418
One continued conversation instead of four separate handoffs.

86
00:03:27,022 --> 00:03:29,216
I could pull that off because I had fifty

87
00:03:29,286 --> 00:03:32,618
people on property and the time to actually think

88
00:03:32,664 --> 00:03:33,303
about it.

89
00:03:33,744 --> 00:03:36,415
A B&B operator running the place alone?

90
00:03:36,623 --> 00:03:37,343
Can't.

91
00:03:38,040 --> 00:03:41,151
That gap, right there, is the whole opportunity.

92
00:03:41,465 --> 00:03:44,867
On the high end: Mews just raised $300 million

93
00:03:44,901 --> 00:03:47,583
in a Series D led by EQT Growth.

94
00:03:48,024 --> 00:03:51,031
Valued the company at $2.5 billion.

95
00:03:51,403 --> 00:03:55,222
Largest funding round hospitality software has ever seen.

96
00:03:55,501 --> 00:03:57,893
Announced January 2026.

97
00:03:58,253 --> 00:04:02,351
New investors Atomico and HarbourVest joined existing backers Kinnevik,

98
00:04:02,490 --> 00:04:04,650
Battery Ventures, and Tiger Global.

99
00:04:05,010 --> 00:04:08,644
Mews now moves $19.7 billion a year

100
00:04:08,899 --> 00:04:12,846
across 42 million bookings and 15,000 customers in 85

101
00:04:12,904 --> 00:04:13,543
countries.

102
00:04:13,961 --> 00:04:18,082
Cloudbeds has raised $250 million, including from SoftBank,

103
00:04:18,593 --> 00:04:21,020
and serves 27,000 properties.

104
00:04:21,623 --> 00:04:24,700
Mews earmarked this round for AI agents that automate

105
00:04:24,746 --> 00:04:26,023
hotel operations.

106
00:04:26,407 --> 00:04:29,239
But somebody still has to install that plumbing in

107
00:04:29,285 --> 00:04:32,420
a 20-room property that's never had a revenue manager

108
00:04:32,455 --> 00:04:33,128
in its life.

109
00:04:33,732 --> 00:04:35,067
The low end's thinner.

110
00:04:35,671 --> 00:04:37,308
And it's in the wrong unit.

111
00:04:37,587 --> 00:04:43,914
Freelance remote hotel revenue managers earn an average $129,482

112
00:04:43,984 --> 00:04:46,259
a year, reported by ZipRecruiter.

113
00:04:46,701 --> 00:04:48,001
But that's a salary.

114
00:04:48,326 --> 00:04:50,067
Not a price you charge a hotel.

115
00:04:50,346 --> 00:04:52,691
So here's the honest state of the evidence: the

116
00:04:52,749 --> 00:04:54,479
demand side is documented.

117
00:04:54,595 --> 00:04:56,186
The pricing side is not.

118
00:04:56,511 --> 00:05:00,366
Providers like TCRM, Revenuenaire, and HotelMinder

119
00:05:00,749 --> 00:05:03,651
report 5 to 15 percent RevPAR uplift.

120
00:05:04,162 --> 00:05:07,180
Their own numbers, unverified, and yeah, they're selling the

121
00:05:07,238 --> 00:05:07,865
service.

122
00:05:08,190 --> 00:05:09,444
What is checkable?

123
00:05:09,769 --> 00:05:12,196
Every single one of them quotes privately.

124
00:05:12,637 --> 00:05:15,807
Nobody in this market publishes a price.

125
00:05:16,317 --> 00:05:18,535
And that's not a hole in the research.

126
00:05:18,558 --> 00:05:19,928
That's the finding.

127
00:05:20,369 --> 00:05:23,202
A market where nobody prices in public...

128
00:05:24,015 --> 00:05:27,719
that's a market a published price can

129
00:05:27,811 --> 00:05:29,506
walk straight into.

130
00:05:30,356 --> 00:05:33,305
The product's a standing task force that generates demand.

131
00:05:34,118 --> 00:05:35,627
The software it runs on?

132
00:05:36,068 --> 00:05:38,274
Assembled, not bought.

133
00:05:39,226 --> 00:05:40,039
Here's the thing.

134
00:05:40,851 --> 00:05:43,812
A booking engine doesn't generate demand on its own.

135
00:05:44,323 --> 00:05:46,436
It catches the guest who's already looking.

136
00:05:47,040 --> 00:05:50,337
Something's gotta keep pushing traffic toward it, every day,

137
00:05:50,511 --> 00:05:53,437
without a payroll line for a full time marketing

138
00:05:53,495 --> 00:05:54,052
hire.

139
00:05:54,377 --> 00:05:57,500
Underneath the task force, two boring pieces.

140
00:05:57,825 --> 00:06:01,657
Make or n8n, that's the orchestration layer.

141
00:06:01,982 --> 00:06:04,269
The thing that runs a sequence on a schedule

142
00:06:04,292 --> 00:06:06,126
without anyone opening a laptop.

143
00:06:06,823 --> 00:06:10,596
And Claude, or any model with an API,

144
00:06:10,712 --> 00:06:12,059
that's the drafting layer.

145
00:06:12,442 --> 00:06:15,124
Neither one's hospitality software, right?

146
00:06:15,937 --> 00:06:16,738
That's the point.

147
00:06:16,761 --> 00:06:19,408
This stack is assembled, not bought.

148
00:06:19,652 --> 00:06:22,543
Then the roles split across the surfaces that actually

149
00:06:22,589 --> 00:06:24,818
decide whether a property gets found.

150
00:06:25,329 --> 00:06:28,115
One agent keeps the Google Business Profile current.

151
00:06:28,440 --> 00:06:29,381
Hours.

152
00:06:29,532 --> 00:06:30,403
Photos.

153
00:06:30,577 --> 00:06:31,296
Rates.

154
00:06:31,807 --> 00:06:34,501
The stuff that goes stale and quietly costs you

155
00:06:34,617 --> 00:06:35,395
rankings.

156
00:06:35,778 --> 00:06:38,599
One runs the re-book flow to past guests through

157
00:06:38,657 --> 00:06:41,629
Beehiiv or Resend, which is the cheapest booking a

158
00:06:41,699 --> 00:06:43,092
hotel's ever gonna get.

159
00:06:43,371 --> 00:06:45,623
One prompts reviews after checkout.

160
00:06:45,948 --> 00:06:47,701
None of them is a receptionist.

161
00:06:48,026 --> 00:06:50,465
Mews or Cloudbeds sits at the end of all

162
00:06:50,523 --> 00:06:53,530
that, and it gets one line here on purpose.

163
00:06:54,226 --> 00:06:57,140
It's the destination, not the product being sold.

164
00:06:57,384 --> 00:07:00,240
The demand the task force generates has to land

165
00:07:00,333 --> 00:07:04,014
somewhere that takes a booking without a commission attached.

166
00:07:04,617 --> 00:07:07,172
And note what the free tier covers.

167
00:07:07,381 --> 00:07:10,864
The Google profile costs nothing, and that's exactly where

168
00:07:10,922 --> 00:07:13,546
most independents are leaking discovery.

169
00:07:13,789 --> 00:07:17,319
Licensed together, the software runs a few hundred bucks

170
00:07:17,354 --> 00:07:18,921
a month, rough estimate.

171
00:07:19,165 --> 00:07:21,126
And that's not the real cost.

172
00:07:21,405 --> 00:07:23,948
Someone still has to direct these agents.

173
00:07:24,122 --> 00:07:25,074
Read the output.

174
00:07:25,225 --> 00:07:26,305
Correct the tone.

175
00:07:26,630 --> 00:07:28,986
Decide what the outreach actually says.

176
00:07:29,370 --> 00:07:31,680
That's the line operators underprice.

177
00:07:31,959 --> 00:07:35,384
And none of the four vendors most often named

178
00:07:35,767 --> 00:07:40,178
for this work, TCRM, Revenuenaire, Catala, HotelMinder,

179
00:07:40,213 --> 00:07:42,477
publish a price you could compare it against.

180
00:07:43,327 --> 00:07:44,221
Okay, so.

181
00:07:44,918 --> 00:07:46,856
Standing this up starts with an audit.

182
00:07:47,031 --> 00:07:48,134
Not a purchase.

183
00:07:48,412 --> 00:07:50,409
Where does a guest first find the property?

184
00:07:51,106 --> 00:07:55,146
Organic search, an OTA listing, a friend's post,

185
00:07:55,227 --> 00:07:56,539
a review site...

186
00:07:56,980 --> 00:07:59,639
and at what point do they fall away instead

187
00:07:59,674 --> 00:08:01,322
of landing on a direct booking.

188
00:08:01,833 --> 00:08:05,595
That gap, mapped honestly, that's what the task

189
00:08:05,641 --> 00:08:07,348
force gets built to close.

190
00:08:07,789 --> 00:08:11,121
Before any agent runs outreach, the destination has to

191
00:08:11,191 --> 00:08:11,888
hold up.

192
00:08:12,398 --> 00:08:14,384
Brand and website first.

193
00:08:14,767 --> 00:08:17,391
Then the full path, from a social post to

194
00:08:17,449 --> 00:08:19,562
the site to a phone call or a booking

195
00:08:19,620 --> 00:08:23,579
to check-in, has to read as one continuous conversation.

196
00:08:24,182 --> 00:08:26,725
Not four disconnected systems.

197
00:08:27,050 --> 00:08:30,022
Then the roles get split, right?

198
00:08:30,719 --> 00:08:33,401
One agent maintains the property's profile.

199
00:08:33,912 --> 00:08:37,638
Photos, descriptions, pricing signals, across every

200
00:08:37,696 --> 00:08:39,554
platform a guest might search.

201
00:08:40,158 --> 00:08:43,246
One runs outreach to past and lookalike guests.

202
00:08:43,850 --> 00:08:48,064
One manages guest engagement, prompting reviews and referrals.

203
00:08:48,668 --> 00:08:50,537
None of them is a receptionist.

204
00:08:51,233 --> 00:08:54,275
At Coqui Coqui I ran this flow myself, because

205
00:08:54,298 --> 00:08:56,167
there was a team of fifty on the property

206
00:08:56,214 --> 00:08:56,910
to lean on.

207
00:08:57,514 --> 00:09:01,078
Someone always available to answer, post, and follow up.

208
00:09:01,589 --> 00:09:05,339
A single-operator bed-and-breakfast doesn't have that headcount.

209
00:09:05,618 --> 00:09:08,648
The agent task force isn't a luxury add-on there.

210
00:09:08,927 --> 00:09:11,574
It's what stands in for the manpower a small

211
00:09:11,644 --> 00:09:13,675
property was never going to hire.

212
00:09:14,000 --> 00:09:18,122
None of the four vendors most commonly named, TCRM,

213
00:09:18,331 --> 00:09:21,861
Revenuenaire, Catala, HotelMinder, post a

214
00:09:21,919 --> 00:09:22,499
price.

215
00:09:22,940 --> 00:09:25,982
Every one quotes privately, after a sales call.

216
00:09:26,191 --> 00:09:28,409
So do the thing none of them does.

217
00:09:28,618 --> 00:09:29,523
Publish yours.

218
00:09:30,127 --> 00:09:33,204
In a market where nobody prices in public, a

219
00:09:33,320 --> 00:09:35,944
number on a website isn't just pricing.

220
00:09:36,547 --> 00:09:37,557
It's the pitch.

221
00:09:37,882 --> 00:09:40,657
Weigh that against the commission line, not the sticker

222
00:09:40,703 --> 00:09:42,190
price of the software.

223
00:09:43,002 --> 00:09:45,858
Licensing runs a few hundred dollars a month, by

224
00:09:45,951 --> 00:09:46,775
rough estimate.

225
00:09:47,159 --> 00:09:49,829
Cheap relative to what it's replacing.

226
00:09:50,270 --> 00:09:53,811
The commission it's meant to offset, on the illustrative

227
00:09:53,846 --> 00:09:57,422
twenty-room model, hundred-eighty-dollar rate, seventy percent

228
00:09:57,468 --> 00:10:01,102
occupancy, runs about one hundred thirty-five thousand dollars

229
00:10:01,137 --> 00:10:01,683
a year.

230
00:10:02,286 --> 00:10:05,758
That gap between those two numbers is the argument.

231
00:10:06,199 --> 00:10:09,253
The pitch isn't to leave the OTAs.

232
00:10:09,578 --> 00:10:12,770
They still bring discovery no small property can buy

233
00:10:12,852 --> 00:10:13,629
on its own.

234
00:10:13,955 --> 00:10:15,580
It's to shift the mix.

235
00:10:15,905 --> 00:10:18,645
Let the task force pull repeat and referred guests

236
00:10:18,715 --> 00:10:21,118
back to the direct channel, one booking at a

237
00:10:21,188 --> 00:10:24,287
time, where no commission gets taken.

238
00:10:25,137 --> 00:10:27,146
There's a full walkthrough of this.

239
00:10:27,471 --> 00:10:31,093
The audit, the pricing, the outreach approach, all

240
00:10:31,163 --> 00:10:33,717
of it, in the blueprint linked below.

241
00:10:34,158 --> 00:10:36,689
For anyone who'd rather start from a built checklist

242
00:10:36,735 --> 00:10:38,988
than piece this together themselves.

243
00:10:39,429 --> 00:10:42,633
And let's talk about the money, on effort, not

244
00:10:42,691 --> 00:10:43,458
promise.

245
00:10:44,061 --> 00:10:46,360
Three or four properties, running this as a side

246
00:10:46,395 --> 00:10:46,824
thing?

247
00:10:47,428 --> 00:10:49,576
That's realistic supplemental income.

248
00:10:50,180 --> 00:10:53,570
At ten or twelve clients you're in freelance revenue-management

249
00:10:53,605 --> 00:10:54,371
territory.

250
00:10:54,812 --> 00:10:58,760
Roughly $95,000 to $140,000 a year.

251
00:10:59,201 --> 00:11:02,661
That's reported salary data for a full-time equivalent, not

252
00:11:02,695 --> 00:11:04,820
what any single hotel is paying you.

253
00:11:05,099 --> 00:11:06,028
But here's the thing.

254
00:11:06,411 --> 00:11:10,126
What this actually diversifies isn't one income stream.

255
00:11:10,451 --> 00:11:12,459
It's skills and optionality.

256
00:11:12,842 --> 00:11:14,433
Revenue-management literacy.

257
00:11:14,712 --> 00:11:17,986
A channel-manager and voice-agent stack you can resell into

258
00:11:18,079 --> 00:11:19,065
other verticals.

259
00:11:19,507 --> 00:11:22,885
A client roster that doesn't disappear just because one

260
00:11:22,931 --> 00:11:27,158
algorithm change wipes out a single OTA relationship.

261
00:11:27,669 --> 00:11:28,992
Costs are real too.

262
00:11:29,375 --> 00:11:31,488
Software runs a few hundred bucks a month per

263
00:11:31,547 --> 00:11:33,358
property, that's my estimate.

264
00:11:33,683 --> 00:11:34,879
And the failure mode?

265
00:11:35,204 --> 00:11:39,035
A hotel signs, sees one good month, and

266
00:11:39,314 --> 00:11:40,335
doesn't renew.

267
00:11:40,776 --> 00:11:43,691
Because nobody proved the number moved.

268
00:11:43,934 --> 00:11:47,371
